  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Jeff McNeil's three-run night kept the offense from looking broken, and that mattered more than the final margin suggests.",
    "what_this_means": "The lineup produced eleven hits but still needed all of them to beat Cleveland by six. Traffic arrived early. Nine strikeouts meant the contact that came had to count. McNeil's bat did. He was the separator between eleven hits and a scattered game, turning the broad activity into actual damage when it mattered most.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is a team that scores in bunches or not at all. When the strikeouts pile up, one player carrying the offensive load is not sustainable. Yet it worked yesterday because Civale kept the game tight enough that seven runs held, and because the rest of the lineup could get on base even when they couldn't drive hard. The formula is: pitch well, let the bats find traffic, then need someone to finish it. McNeil finished it. The margin held only because all three parts aligned.",
